<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="Sx5.T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="Sx5.T4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A one-dimensional subject–verb agreement core was extracted from each GPT-2 model scale (Small, Medium, and Large),
despite variation in training, model parameterizations, and architectures (model dimension, number of layers).
Extracted core size (<math id="Sx5.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\rm core}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>core</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\rm core}</annotation></semantics></math>) is supported by the large <em id="Sx5.T4.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">spectral gap</em> (ratio of largest two singular value squares <math id="Sx5.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}^{2}/\sigma_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}^{2}/\sigma_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The large spectral gaps indicate that these subspaces are effectively one-dimensional.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
